                <pb facs="0556" n="444" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg444"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7432: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-26T15:00:00+08:00">November 26, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 26—9:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>111. 1. According to information received from the Foreign Office the Chinese
                    Government, following a special meeting of the Executive Yuan today, has (1)
                    ordered local authorities in the North to arrest Yin Ju-keng as a rebel, (2)
                    abolished the Peiping Branch Military Council, (3) appointed Ho Ying-chin as its
                    resident representative in Peiping, and (4) Sung Che-yuan as Pacification
                    Commissioner for Hopei and Chahar. The warrant for Yin’s arrest, it is stated,
                    will be issued tomorrow morning.</p>
                <p>2. Last three steps have the appearance of being a form of compliance with the
                    suggestion made to Chiang by the Japanese as reported in my [103,] November 22,
                    10 a.m.,<anchor n="53" xml:id="d398-f1"/> paragraph 2, first sentence.</p>
                <p>3. Repeated to Department and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
